@inject NavigationManager Navigation

@* The line above the card on every page that lists what a store holds — not the table's own head
   row, which the table writes itself. What the page found at one end, and the ways of asking the
   store for it again at the other, in the same shape as the pager and the rows-per-page picker at
   the two ends of the line below the card.

   Outside the card rather than in it, because both halves are the page speaking about the table
   rather than part of it: the count says what was found, and the refresh asks for the whole thing
   again. Inside, they would also have prised the filter row off the table it narrows, and those
   two have to sit against each other to read as one object.

   One component for the whole line rather than a wrapper repeated on all six pages, so the count
   and the controls cannot drift apart on one of them. *@
<div class="table-header">
    @* Drawn only when there is something to count. The pages hand in nothing rather than a sentence
       when there are no rows — a count of none is what the empty table below already says, at more
       length and in the right place. The line stays either way, because a table with nothing in it
       is exactly the one worth asking about again. *@
    @if (Summary is not null)
    {
        <p class="lede">@Summary</p>
    }

    @* Nothing without scripting, so it is not there without it: a timer is the one control on these
       pages that cannot fall back to a link. Rendered hidden and shown by the script that drives it,
       the way the copy button on a payload is. *@
    <div class="auto-refresh" data-preference-row="auto-refresh" hidden>
        <label for="auto-refresh">Auto refresh</label>
        @* No name, so it is never submitted by anything it might one day sit inside: the choice is
           this browser's and the server has never heard of it, like the theme. Off is what is
           rendered every time for the same reason, and the script puts back what was picked.

           The same list the preferences page offers, from <see cref="RefreshInterval"/>: one
           preference under two labels, the way the rows-per-page picker is. *@
        <select id="auto-refresh" data-preference="auto-refresh">
            <option value="" selected>Off</option>
            @foreach (var interval in RefreshInterval.Offered)
            {
                <option value="@interval.Seconds">@interval.Label</option>
            }
        </select>
    </div>

    @* A link back to the address the page is already at, like the order, the page and the fold: the
       whole of what is being looked at is in the address, so asking for that address again is asking
       for exactly this table again, narrowing, order, page and all.

       A link rather than a button with script behind it buys three things at once — it works with
       scripting off, it can be opened in a new tab, and under enhanced navigation Blazor swaps the
       table in place without reloading the document or losing the scroll.

       The mark alone, with no word beside it, the way the Json links in the table below are drawn:
       this is the one drawing a reader arrives already knowing. Named for a screen reader and titled
       for a pointer, because a mark on its own says nothing to either. *@
    <a class="refresh-now" href="@Here" data-refresh title="Refresh" aria-label="Refresh">
        <RefreshMark/>
    </a>
</div>

@code {

    /// <summary>
    /// What the page found, in its own words — the whole count rather than this page's worth, since
    /// the pager beneath already says which page. Null where there is nothing to say, which leaves
    /// the line holding the controls alone rather than taking it away.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Summary { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The address this page is being read at, relative to the base so it is written the way every
    /// other link here is. The query string comes with it, which is the whole point: it carries the
    /// narrowing, the order, the page and the fold.
    /// </summary>
    private string Here => Navigation.ToBaseRelativePath(Navigation.Uri);

}
